# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All Clear — dashboard configuration (staging: the real product)
# Copy to dashboard/.env.local and fill in. .env*.local is gitignored.
#
# In production these are set in the Vercel dashboard, not in a file. Paste
# secrets straight into Vercel; never into a commit, a chat, or a shared doc.
#
# Everything listed here is read by code on this branch. If you add a variable,
# add it here in the same commit — a variable the code reads and the example
# omits is the exact defect that broke signed URLs (BUILD_CONTEXT D1).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Supabase (browser-safe — RLS applies) ───────────────────────────────────
# Read by lib/supabase/{client,server,middleware}.ts and api/pilot-request.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ── Supabase service role (server-side only — NEVER browser-safe) ───────────
#
# NEW IN PHASE 3. Read by lib/supabase/service-role.ts, which is imported only
# by lib/device-auth.ts and the /api/v1/ device routes.
#
# ⚠ THIS KEY BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY COMPLETELY. It is the Supabase
# equivalent of a database superuser. Anyone holding it can read and write
# every row of every organisation.
#
#   - No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, ever. That prefix is what inlines a value into
#     the browser bundle, and this value in a bundle is a total breach.
#   - service-role.ts starts with `import "server-only"`, so importing it from
#     a Client Component is a BUILD failure rather than a runtime leak.
#   - Paste it straight into Vercel. Never into a commit, a chat, or a doc.
#
# Why the dashboard needs it now, when it did not before: a device
# authenticates with its own API key, not a Supabase session, so there is no
# RLS identity to attach to a device request and RLS cannot be the control on
# those routes. Application logic is. In exchange, Step 3.4 removes this key
# from the Python detection service entirely — net, it lives on one server we
# control instead of on every field device.
#
# Find it at: Supabase Dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role secret
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── S3 (server-side only — /api/signed-url) ─────────────────────────────────
#
# S3_*, never AWS_*. Vercel functions run on AWS Lambda, and the Lambda runtime
# presets AWS_REGION to the function's own region and AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID /
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY to execution-role placeholders that grant no access to
# our bucket. Because those presets are always present they beat any fallback
# written in code, so a client built from AWS_* silently points at the wrong
# region with useless credentials and fails at request time, not deploy time.
#
# Some AWS_* names cannot even be set in the Vercel dashboard — AWS_SECRET_KEY,
# AWS_EXECUTION_ENV, AWS_LAMBDA_*, NOW_REGION, TZ, LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT are
# reserved. Our own namespace avoids the whole category.
#
# detection/.env.example uses these same names for the same reason.
#
S3_REGION=ca-central-1
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Company account buckets, ca-central-1. Use -dev locally, -prod on Vercel.
# The old cordon-safety-violations-dev bucket is retired.
#
# Also read by next.config.ts at build time, for the image remotePatterns
# allowlist and the CSP img-src directive. A wrong value there surfaces as a
# blocked image with a CSP console error, not as a 403 — so check this first
# when snapshots stop rendering.
S3_BUCKET_NAME=allclear-violations-dev

# ── Rate limiting needs no variables ────────────────────────────────────────
# RESOLVED 2026-08-28 (BUILD_CONTEXT D6). The durable limiter is a Postgres
# counter — check_rate_limit() and rate_limit_counters, from migration 006 —
# so it runs on the database connection above and needs nothing here.
#
# Upstash was rejected: a new vendor for one counter, and its free tier is
# tight against 30-second heartbeats from every device. Do NOT add
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL / UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN; nothing reads them.
#
# lib/rate-limit.ts keeps its in-memory Map as a free per-instance pre-filter
# in front of the Postgres counter. It is not the limit; it can only undercount
# relative to the durable layer, which is what makes short-circuiting on it safe.
